Zeile 0: 0.05-2.23  Why you should never buy a bridge from a stranger.
Zeile 1: 2.33-7.45  In New York, one con man sold the Brooklyn Bridge. Not once. Over and over again.
Zeile 2: 7.55-9.42  He picked people who were new in town.
Zeile 3: 9.52-12.37  And showed them forged papers saying he owned the bridge.
Zeile 4: 12.47-15.91  His pitch: charge a toll on every wagon, and get rich.
Zeile 5: 16.01-20.28  One buyer paid a thousand dollars in cash, and promised four thousand more.
Zeile 6: 20.38-25.13  Some buyers even started building toll booths, until the police chased them off.
Zeile 7: 25.23-28.95  In 1928, the con man got life in Sing Sing prison.
Zeile 8: 29.05-30.92  Would you have bought it?
